<i data-commentator="Be'er HaGolah" data-label="ה" data-order="5"></i> On the third Shabbos, which is the fifteenth of Adar, we interrupt [certain study]. On the fourth Shabbos, which is the twenty-second of Adar, we take out two scrolls: from the first we read that week’s regular <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="ט"></i> portion, and from the second we read <i data-commentator="Eshel Avraham" data-label="א" data-order="1"></i>&lt;i&gt;Parashas Parah&lt;/i&gt;,<i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="י"></i> and the haftarah is &lt;i&gt;Uzerakti aleichem mayim tehorim&lt;/i&gt; &lt;small&gt;[and see below siman 137 seif 4]&lt;/small&gt;:<small> <i data-commentator="Mishnah Berurah" data-label="יא"></i>[']</small>:
